00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
And open source ai movie just dropped with real celebrity faces you can remix yourself. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 130. A filmmaker released the first full feature length movie created entirely by ai. The project secured legal licenses for real celebrity likenesses before any generation began. Every frame, every line of dialogue, and every sound came from ai tools. The creators then released all 473,000

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:59.580
600 assets under an open source license. Think of it like handing over the complete recipe. Every ingredient measured out, and a video of the entire baking process, so anyone can recreate or tweak the cake. In this case, the cake is a 2 hour film, and the ingredients include digital versions of actors, faces, and voices. Movie making used to demand massive crews, sound stages, and years of coordination. Now one person can guide the ai through the whole story,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:18.460
while the tools handle the visuals and audio. That shift matters for students who want to tell stories or create art, because the cost barrier just fell sharply. The open release also lets anyone study exactly how the ai kept lighting consistent, and characters recognizable across thousands of frames.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:47.340
Go to the project page linked from the post on x by Linus Eckenstom. Scroll to the asset section, and download a few small files like a character model or background scene. Open them in any free viewer and notice the details the ai had to solve. The explain like I'm 14 section this week focuses on something called watermarking. You know how a teacher sometimes adds a small note at the bottom of a group project listing every contributor so no one can claim sole credit later.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:02:03.280
Now picture that note written in a secret code that only computers can read. Every time Claude generates text, it weaves two tiny hidden marks into the output itself. One mark sits inside the actual words so it survives copying into another document.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:17.180
The second mark uses an open standard called C2PA that travels along with image or video files and records which ai created them. These marks stay invisible to human eyes and do not change how the text reads.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:31.560
Websites and apps can later scan for the marks to check whether something came from Claude. It works like slipping a tiny invisible stamp onto every page that quietly says made by anthropic, unless someone is actively looking for it.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:41.900
The system began rolling out worldwide this month and will appear in every new Claude model from the start. older models will receive the same treatment over the next few weeks.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:54.020
Anthropic also plans to release free detection tools so anyone can verify the marks on their own. The policy applies across the main website, the API, and third party platforms.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:05.680
Because the marks are added at the model level, they appear automatically no matter how the output is created. This approach gives a consistent way to trace ai text even after light editing.

